args=[\"test requirement\"]. RunE: not --job (jobID empty), GetDryRun() true → handler.PlanDryRun(). Returns nil (PlanDryRun prints). Good.\n\n  `TestPlanCmdWithJobFlagDryRun`: dryRun=true, jobID=\"job_1\", args=[\"some requirement\"]. RunE: existingJobID != \"\" and GetDryRun() → prints \"[DRY-RUN] Would re-enter plan...\" returns nil. Good.\n\n- `TestReEnterPlanWorkflow_NonExistentJob` — references `reEnterPlanWorkflow`. Rewrite to call `handler.ReEnterPlan(\"job_999\", \"test requirement\", opts)`. But this test does `os.Chdir(dir)` and creates `.rick`. handler.ReEnterPlan uses workspace.GetJobPlanDir + os.Stat + config.LoadConfig. With chdir to temp dir with .rick, GetJobPlanDir returns `<cwd>/.rick/jobs/job_999/plan`. It checks os.Stat → not exist → error \"job job_999 plan directory does not exist\". Good, no HOME needed (LoadConfig only called after the stat check, and the stat fails first). Actually wait: reEnterPlanWorkflow checks `os.Stat(jobPlanDir)` BEFORE LoadConfig. So it returns the error before LoadConfig. Good, no HOME pollution.\n\n- `TestExecutePlanWorkflow_WithMockPi` — references `executePlanWorkflow`. Rewrite to `handler.Plan(\"test requirement\", handler.Options{Verbose: false})`. Actually it needs to set up mock pi. Let me look: it sets HOME, writes config with pi_path mock, sets PATH, then calls executePlanWorkflow. The migrated handler.Plan would do the same flow. I'll rewrite to call handler.Plan. But note the original test used GetVerbose() which reads package var verbose=false. handler.Plan needs opts.Verbose. Since verbose default false, pass Options{Verbose:false}.\n\n**doing_test.go** (package cmd):\n- `TestExtractTaskNumber` — extractTaskNumber moved to handler (unexported). Move this test to handler package, OR remove. I'll move to handler test file (handler/doing_test.go).\n- `TestSortTaskFiles` — same, move to handler.\n- `TestLoadTasksFromPlan`, `TestLoadTasksFromPlanNoFiles`, `TestLoadTasksFromPlanNotFound` — move to handler.\n-