{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I have all the info I need. Let me start implementing.\n\nFirst, let me write the handler package files.\n\n### 1. internal/handler/options.go\n\n```go\npackage handler\n\n// Options carries flag values from the CLI layer into handler orchestrators.\n// Handlers must not import internal/cmd (that would create a cycle); the cmd\n// layer resolves flags and passes them here.\ntype Options struct {\n\tVerbose bool\n\tDryRun  bool\n\tJobID   string\n}\n```\n\n### 2. internal/handler/plan.go\n\nMigrate executePlanWorkflow → Plan, reEnterPlanWorkflow → ReEnterPlan, runPlanDryRun → PlanDryRun.\n\nReplace GetVerbose() with opts.Verbose. The original executePlanWorkflow used `GetVerbose()` 5 times, and `builder.NewPIBuilder().SavePlanPrompt`. Also `runtime.CallCLI(GetVerbose(), cfg, planPromptFile, runtime.ModeInteractive)`.\n\nNote: Plan doesn't use DryRun or JobID (those branches remain in cmd). But to keep a consistent signature, I'll define `Plan(requirement string, opts Options) error` and only use opts.Verbose.\n\nReEnterPlan uses verbose. PlanDryRun doesn't use verbose.\n\nLet me write plan.go.\n\n### 3. internal/handler/doing.go\n\nMigrate executeDoingWorkflow → Doing, runDoingDryRun → DoingDryRun, plus helpers.\n\nDoing(jobID string, opts Options) error uses opts.Verbose.\n\nDoingDryRun(jobID string) error — original didn't use verbose.\n\nHelpers: loadTasksFromPlan, sortTaskFiles, extractTaskNumber, printExecutionSummary (unexported), commitDoingResults(jobID, result, verbose), ensureGitUserConfigured(projectRoot, verbose), ensureGitInitialized(rickDir, verbose).\n\n### 4. internal/handler/easy.go\n\nMigrate runEasyMode → Easy, resumeEasyMode → ResumeEasy, startEasySession → StartEasySession, runEasyDryRun → EasyDryRun, plus validateCtxInheritance, saveSessionID, loadSessionID, writeEasyTasksJSON, generateUUID.\n\nImportant: `runEasyMode` currently prompts when requirement empty. In the migrated `Easy`, I should NOT prompt (prompt stays in cmd). But I need to keep the empty-requirement validation.\n\nWait, but let me think about the flow again. Original runEasyMode:\n```go\nfunc runEasyMode(requirement, ctxPath string) error {\n\tif requirement == \"\" {\n\t\trequirement, err = promptForRequirement()  // prompts\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\tif requirement == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"requirement cannot be empty\")\n\t}\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nAfter migration, cmd/easy.go RunE will resolve requirement (including prompt) before calling handler.Easy. So handler.Easy only needs:\n```go\nfunc Easy(requirement, ctxPath string, opts Options) error {\n\tif requirement == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"requirement cannot be empty\")\n\t}\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nAnd `EasyDryRun(requirement, ctxPath string) error`:\n```go\nfunc EasyDryRun(requirement, ctxPath string) error {\n\tif requirement == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"requirement cannot be empty\")\n\t}\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nWait — but the test expects `easy --dry-run -r \"\"` to report \"requirement cannot be empty\". If EasyDryRun validates empty → returns error. Good.\n\nBut note: the original `runEasyDryRun` did NOT validate empty requirement. Now I'm adding validation. This is a behavior change, but it's exactly what the test method requires. Good.\n\nAlso note: `BuildEasy` already substitutes \"<requirement>\" for empty, but now we intercept empty before building.\n\nNow, the `StartEasySession` signature. Original:\n```go\nfunc startEasySession(jobID, requirement, rickDir string, cfg *config.Config, ctxPath string) error\n```\nuses GetVerbose() in `runtime.CallCLI(GetVerbose(), cfg, mainFile, ...)`. Migrated:\n```go\nfunc StartEasySession(jobID, requirement, rickDir string, cfg *config.Config, ctxPath string, opts Options) error\n```\nuses opts.Verbose.\n\n`ResumeEasy(jobID string, opts Options) error` — uses GetVerbose() in CallCLI and loadSessionID, writeEasyTasksJSON.\n\n`Easy(requirement, ctxPath string, opts Options) error` — calls StartEasySession with opts. Also validateCtxInheritance.\n\nNow let me write the code.\n\nLet me also double check the config import in handler: `config.Config` and `config.LoadConfig()`.\n\nNow, one concern: the handler package imports `internal/executor`, `internal/git`, `internal/parser`, `internal/builder`, `internal/config`, `internal/runtime`, `internal/workspace`. None of these import cmd. So no cycle.\n\nLet me verify parser.Task fields (ID, Name, Goal, KeyResults, TestMethod, Dependencies)."}}
