skStruct, TestExecutionJobResultDuration.\n\nWait, but TestDoingCmdWithJobIDArg: it calls `cmd.RunE(cmd, []string{\"job1\"})`. With the new RunE, jobID = args[0] = \"job1\", not dry-run, validateJobID passes, then handler.Doing(\"job1\", opts). handler.Doing does LoadConfig etc. It will fail at workspace or job directory not found, but the test only asserts the error is not the \"job ID is required\" message. So it passes (error is something else). But wait — handler.Doing calls `ensureGitInitialized(rickDir, opts.Verbose)` which calls `os.Getwd()` and checks `.git`. In the test env (repo root has .git), it'll be \"already initialized\". Then LoadConfig reads real config? The test doesn't set HOME. Hmm, TestDoingCmdWithJobIDArg currently passes because executeDoingWorkflow was called which also did LoadConfig with real HOME. Wait, but actually the current test passed before. Let me check: `cmd.RunE(cmd, []string{\"job1\"})` — the old code also called executeDoingWorkflow(\"job1\") which did LoadConfig (real ~/.rick/config.json). So it read real config. That's existing behavior and it passed (it's just asserting error != \"job ID is required\"). So fine.\n\nActually wait, is there a concern that handler.Doing might hang or do network? No, it does LoadConfig, workspace.New, GetRickDir, ensureGitInitialized (no-op since .git exists), then checks jobDir `./.rick/jobs/job1` doesn't exist → returns \"job directory not found\". So it's fast. Good.\n\nTestDoingCmdMissingJobID: RunE with no args, no --job → jobID empty → dry-run false → jobID == \"\" → returns \"job ID is required...\". Good, still passes.\n\nNow, for the moved tests, I'll create handler/doing_test.go.\n\nLet me write the new cmd/doing_test.go first."}}
